[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0mThe translation is correct. I found no behavioral mismatch between the C and Rust programs across roughly 145,000 differential inputs.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mWhat I did[22m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Built both programs ([38;5;10mcmake -S c_src -B build && cmake --build build[0m; [38;5;10mcargo build --release[0m), then wrote a differential test suite in [38;5;10mtranslation/tests/[0m that spawns both binaries as subprocesses, feeds identical bytes on stdin, and compares stdout, stderr and exit status. 27 tests, none ignored or disabled, passing in both the debug and release profiles.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mCoverage[22m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
I enumerated the C's branches rather than guessing at them: all three [38;5;10mfgets[0m failure paths, the [38;5;10mlength == 0[0m and [38;5;10mlength < 3[0m guards, every operation including the unhandled ones, all four logic operators over all eight condition triples, every [38;5;10my[0m/[38;5;10mn[0m string of length 1–13 for operations 2 and 3, the 32-decision cap, the 1023-byte [38;5;10mfgets[0m truncation in each of the three input positions, and [38;5;10matoi[0m across the [38;5;10mint[0m and [38;5;10mlong[0m boundaries. A golden table pins one input per reachable [38;5;10mreturn[0m in the C, with an assertion that the corpus still spans every result value — this is what caught the one real defect I hit, a gap in my own test corpus rather than in the translation.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mWorth knowing[22m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Four [38;5;10mreturn[0m statements in the C are unreachable for any input (the [38;5;10mread && write[0m fall-through in [38;5;10mapply_permissions[0m, [38;5;10m90[0m in the XOR arm, [38;5;10m100[0m in the NAND arm, and [38;5;10m40[0m in [38;5;10mvalidate_sequence[0m's long band). I proved each unreachable by enumeration and left the Rust's code shape mirroring the C anyway.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Because [38;5;10mvalidate_sequence[0m casts [38;5;10mchar*[0m to [38;5;10mbool*[0m, the C is technically a strict-aliasing violation and so could in principle be optimization-sensitive. I rebuilt it at [38;5;10m-O0[0m, [38;5;10m-O2[0m and [38;5;10m-O3[0m and cross-compared all three against each other and against the Rust; they agree, so this isn't a live hazard.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The riskiest spot in the translation is [38;5;10matoi[0m, which [38;5;10mmain.rs[0m reimplements by hand as glibc's [38;5;10m(int) strtol(s, NULL, 10)[0m — saturating at [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m and [3mthen[23m truncating to [38;5;10mint[0m. That's why [38;5;10m4294967296[0m selects operation 0 and [38;5;10m9223372036854775808[0m becomes [38;5;10m-1[0m. Rust's [38;5;10mstr::parse[0m would have rejected all of these. I verified it against ~5,600 numeric inputs specifically.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Findings, the C behaviors that are easy to get wrong, and the unreachability proofs are written up in [38;5;10mtranslation/ERRORS.md[0m. [38;5;10mc_src/[0m is untouched — the test harness builds the C into [38;5;10mtranslation/target/c_build[0m, so [38;5;10mcargo test[0m never writes there.[0m[0m
